{"exports":["def query(sql: str, tables: dict[str, list[dict]]) -> list[dict] - Executes `sql` against the in-memory `tables` mapping by calling parser.parse(sql), planner.plan(select, tables) and engine.execute(plan, tables) in that order, and returns the result rows as a list of dicts (one per output row, in result order, keyed by the output column names defined in the spec's Output naming section); raises minidb.errors.QueryError for any malformed query, unknown table, unknown column, ambiguous unqualified column, type error, mixed-type ORDER BY comparison or negative LIMIT/OFFSET, and never lets KeyError, IndexError, AttributeError, TypeError, ValueError, ZeroDivisionError, RecursionError or any other non-QueryError exception escape (unexpected exceptions are caught and re-raised as QueryError with the original exception chained via `raise ... from exc`)."],"module":"api","notes":"Public surface: `query` is the ONLY public name in minidb/api.py; minidb/__init__.py (provided by the harness) re-exports `query` from here and `QueryError` from minidb/errors.py, so dependents must import QueryError from minidb.errors, not from api.\n\nInputs: `tables` is not copied or mutated; keys are table names (case-sensitive), values are lists of row dicts mapping column name to value. A table's column order is the key order of its first row; an empty table list therefore contributes no columns to `*` expansion. `sql` keywords are case-insensitive, identifiers case-sensitive. Passing anything other than a str for `sql` or a dict of lists of dicts for `tables` is reported as QueryError, not TypeError.\n\nOutputs: a new list of new dicts is returned; callers may mutate it freely. Values are the Python values taken from the input rows or computed by the engine (None represents SQL NULL; AVG yields float; division by zero yields None). An aggregate select list with no GROUP BY always yields exactly one row, even over zero input rows; otherwise zero result rows yield an empty list.\n\nError behaviour: api performs no validation of its own beyond the argument-shape checks and the catch-all wrapping described above; all diagnostics originate in tokens/parser/planner/engine, and their QueryError messages are propagated unchanged (message text is not part of the contract, so callers must branch on the exception type only). KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit are BaseException subclasses and propagate untouched.\n\nDependency direction: api depends on parser.parse, planner.plan, engine.execute and errors.QueryError exactly as those modules publish them, and no module imports api, so adding api introduces no import cycle."}